---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";

const mint = `import { generateSigner, mintToken } from "@statewalker/httpeers-access/issuer";

const hub = await generateSigner();          // hub.mesh is the hub's peer id: the mesh's name
const token = await mintToken({
  signer: hub,
  sub: alicePeerId,                          // the member the token is for
  roles: ["member"],
  ttlMs: 10 * 60_000,
  // audience: [bobPeerId],                  // optional: the peers it may be presented to
});`;

const authority = `mesh("<hub peer id>"); subject("<alice>"); bound("<alice>");
role("member"); issued_at(<ms>); expires_at(<ms>);
audience_unrestricted(true);                 // or: audience("<peer>"); ...
check if bound($k), connection_peer($k);     // only over a connection that proved <alice>
check if mesh($m), root_mesh($m);            // only in the mesh whose key verified it
check if audience_unrestricted(true);        // or: check if audience($k), self_peer($k);
check if time_ms($t), $t < <expires ms>;`;

const verifier = `connection_peer("<the peer the transport proved>");
self_peer("<this peer's own id>");          // when withAccess is given selfPeer
root_mesh("<the mesh this peer expects>");
time_ms(<this peer's clock>);
allow if true;                              // no policy here: the token's checks decide`;

const guard = `import { access, ruleSet, withAccess } from "@statewalker/httpeers-access";

const rules = ruleSet({
  version: 1,
  rules: [
    'capability("app:read")  <- role("member");',
    'capability("app:write") <- role("admin");',
    'role("member")          <- role("admin");',
  ],
  policies: [
    'allow if capability("app:read"), resource($r), $r.starts_with("/data");',
    'allow if capability("app:write"), resource($r), $r.starts_with("/admin");',
  ],
});

const guard = withAccess({
  issuer: hubPeerId,                         // whose tokens are accepted
  rules,
  selfPeer: myPeerId,
  provenPeer: (req) => lookupPeer(req) ?? ANONYMOUS,
});
// inside a handler: access(req)?.claims, access(req)?.capabilities()`;

const attenuate = `import { Biscuit } from "@statewalker/webrun-biscuit";

// Alice, holding her token: no hub, no key of the hub's.
const narrowed = Biscuit.fromBase64(token)
  .attenuate("check if self_peer({bob}); check if time_ms($t), $t < {until};", {
    params: { bob: bobPeerId, until: Date.now() + 30_000 },
  })
  .toBase64();`;
---

<Docs
  title="Access control"
  description="How a peer decides who may call it: Biscuit membership tokens verified against the hub's peer id, a Datalog policy held by each peer, attenuation, peer binding, revocation and expiry."
>
  <h1>Access control</h1>
  <p>
    Every peer decides for itself who may call it. A <strong>hub</strong> signs membership tokens; the peer being
    called verifies the token, binds it to the caller the transport proved, and evaluates its <em>own</em> policy.
    No server is asked at request time. This page describes what
    <a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-access/README.md")}>httpeers-access</a> and
    <a href={docHref("packages/webrun-biscuit/README.md")}>webrun-biscuit</a> implement, and says plainly what
    they do not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tokens">A membership token is a Biscuit, verified with the hub's public key</h2>
  <p>
    A token is a <a href="https://www.biscuitsec.org/">Biscuit</a>: a chain of signed blocks whose content is Datalog
    facts and checks, serialised as URL-safe base64. The first block, the <em>authority</em> block, is signed with
    the hub's Ed25519 key. Verification needs only the matching public key, so there is no shared secret: a peer
    that verifies tokens cannot mint them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The public key comes from the mesh's name. A mesh is named by its hub's libp2p peer id, and an Ed25519 peer id
    contains its public key, so <code>verifyToken</code> recovers the key by parsing the id
    (<code>selfCertifyingKeys</code>). No key directory, no fetch. An RSA peer id hashes its key; nothing can be
    recovered from it, and its tokens are refused (<code>issuer-not-ed25519</code>).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={mint} lang="ts" title="hub side: @statewalker/httpeers-access/issuer" />
  <p>
    <code>mintToken</code> writes one authority block. Every value is bound as a Datalog parameter, never spliced
    into the source, so a role named <code>"); role("admin</code> is just a role with an odd name:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={authority} lang="txt" title="the authority block mintToken writes" />
  <p>
    The verifying side is a separate entry point: a member imports <code>@statewalker/httpeers-access</code> and
    never pulls minting into its bundle; a hub imports <code>./issuer</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy">The token states facts; the called peer states its policy</h2>
  <p>
    Two Datalog evaluations decide a request, and they are kept apart on purpose.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Verification</strong> (<code>verifyToken</code>). The peer checks the signature chain, then asserts only
      facts it knows itself, never anything read from the token:
    </li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock code={verifier} lang="txt" title="what the verifier asserts" />
  <p>
    The token's own checks run against those facts. A failed check refuses the token with a stable
    <code>reason</code>: <code>peer-binding</code>, <code>audience</code>, <code>expired</code>,
    <code>mesh-mismatch</code>, <code>signature</code>, or <code>unsatisfied-constraint</code> for a check the
    verifier cannot satisfy (an unknown predicate denies). Evaluation is bounded (<code>LIMITS</code>: 5,000 facts,
    200 iterations, 1 s), so a pathological token denies rather than stalling the peer.
  </p>
  <ol start="2">
    <li>
      <strong>Policy</strong> (<code>withPolicy</code>, inside <code>withAccess</code>). From the verified claims the
      peer takes the <code>role</code> facts, adds its own <code>operation</code>, <code>resource</code>,
      <code>time_ms</code>, <code>self_peer</code> and <code>connection_peer</code>, derives
      <code>capability</code> facts with its <code>RuleSet</code>, and runs its policies, <code>deny</code> before
      <code>allow</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock code={guard} lang="ts" title="a peer's guard" />
  <p>
    The rule set is built locally and never fetched, so nothing remote can widen a decision. There is no default
    one. <code>ruleSet()</code> refuses to build a policy that could never match (a predicate nothing asserts, a
    capability no rule derives) and lists every problem, because a policy that silently denies everyone looks
    exactly like one that works. A request with no token, or one the caller can fix by presenting a fresh token
    over the right connection (<code>expired</code>, <code>malformed-token</code>, <code>peer-binding</code>), is a
    <code>401</code>; every other refusal, a valid token without the capability included, is a <code>403</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The token travels in <code>x-httpeers-token</code>, never in <code>Authorization</code>, which stays the
    application's (<a href="/docs/architecture#headers">the two headers</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="attenuation">Attenuation: a holder can narrow a token, never widen it</h2>
  <p>
    Biscuit lets whoever holds a token append a block, signed with a key that travels inside the token, without
    the hub. <code>webrun-biscuit</code> implements it (<code>Biscuit.attenuate</code>, and <code>seal()</code> to
    forbid further blocks). An appended block can add <strong>checks</strong>, and every check of every block must
    pass. It cannot add <strong>rights</strong>: a check in the authority block, and the claims
    <code>verifyToken</code> reads back, see only authority and verifier facts, so an appended
    <code>role("admin")</code> or <code>audience("elsewhere")</code> is invisible to them.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={attenuate} lang="ts" title="narrowing a token" />
  <p>
    What an appended check can usefully constrain is limited by what the verifier asserts:
    <code>self_peer</code> (which peer accepts it), <code>time_ms</code> (a shorter life) and
    <code>connection_peer</code>. The policy's <code>resource</code> and <code>operation</code> facts are not
    asserted during verification, so a block that checks a path (<code>check if resource("/data")</code>) fails
    everywhere: the token is refused outright, not narrowed to that path. Narrowing a token to a path or a method is
    <strong>not supported</strong> today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="passing">Passing a right to another peer, and checking it there</h2>
  <p>
    A token is presented with each request, and the receiving peer checks it on arrival. Alice can narrow her token
    and send it to Bob: Bob verifies the chain against the hub's peer id, asserts his own facts, and accepts the
    token only if every check, Alice's included, holds for him. The same token sent to Dave fails Alice's
    <code>self_peer</code> check there. The <a href="/examples/delegation">narrowing example</a> runs exactly that,
    plus a widening attempt and a tampered token.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is <strong>not supported</strong> is handing a right to a different holder. The authority block binds the
    token to its subject (next section), and an appended block cannot change the subject, so Alice cannot give Carol
    a token Carol can use. Delegation to another member is listed under <em>Not implemented</em> in
    httpeers-access; Carol needs a token of her own from the hub.
  </p>

  <h3 id="application-grants">Rights an application passes on</h3>
  <p>
    Rights inside an application can travel. <code>Authorization</code> passes through the mesh untouched
    (<a href="/docs/architecture#headers">the two headers</a>), so an application can sign its own Biscuits with
    its own key and its own vocabulary. Alice attenuates the grant Bob's application gave her, for example to
    <code>check if operation("GET"); check if caller("&lt;carol&gt;");</code>, and hands it to Carol by any
    channel. Bob's handler verifies it against the application's public key and asserts the caller the transport
    proved (<code>lookupPeer</code>), which lets a block bind the right to a peer: because of Alice's
    <code>check if caller(…)</code>, Carol may read, may not write, and Alice cannot use Carol's copy. The membership
    token still decides whether Carol may reach Bob at all; the application token decides what she may do there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The grant Alice received is a bearer token.</strong> It names no holder, because a holder check in it
    would also bind every copy Alice hands on (each block's checks must all hold). Whoever obtains it may use it,
    so give it a short life (<code>check if time_ms($t), $t &lt; …</code>, with the handler asserting
    <code>time_ms</code>) and add a caller check in every block that passes it on. The
    <a href="/examples/app-grants">application grant example</a> runs exactly that. Verifying the grant is the
    handler's code, not httpeers-access's: the library checks membership only.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hub can also restrict a token to named peers when it mints it (<code>audience</code>). That restriction sits
    in the signed authority block and is evaluated by the destination against its own <code>self_peer</code>, so a
    peer that is not in the audience refuses however the request reached it. The hub's invitation and heartbeat
    endpoints mint unrestricted tokens today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="binding">A token is bound to its peer: Carol's token in Alice's hands is refused</h2>
  <p>
    The authority block says <code>bound("&lt;sub&gt;")</code> and <code>check if bound($k), connection_peer($k)</code>.
    The verifier asserts <code>connection_peer</code> from what the <em>transport</em> proved about the other end
    (the Noise handshake in libp2p; the link itself in the in-tab examples), never from a header or the token. So
    when Alice presents Carol's token, <code>connection_peer</code> is Alice, the check fails, and the request is
    refused with <code>peer-binding</code> (401). A stolen token is useless to anyone but its subject, and the check
    lives in the token, so it holds even at a verifier that forgot to compare the two itself. A request with no
    proven peer asserts no <code>connection_peer</code> at all and fails the same way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="revocation">Expiry is in the token; revocation is a list each peer pulls</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Expiry.</strong> Every token carries <code>check if time_ms($t), $t &lt; &lt;exp&gt;</code> with the bound
    inlined, evaluated against the verifying peer's clock. Members do not hold long-lived tokens: each presence
    heartbeat to the hub (<code>POST /.well-known/presence</code>) returns a freshly minted one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Revocation.</strong> A token states its roles until it expires, so removing or demoting a member takes a
    revocation entry: <code>hub.revocations.changeRoles(peerId, roles)</code> (empty roles removes the member). The
    hub publishes the list at <code>/.well-known/revocations</code>; each member pulls it when the heartbeat reports a
    new policy version and keeps it in a <code>RevocationCache</code>. A token minted before the change
    (<code>iat &lt; changedAt</code>, both hub timestamps, so no clock synchronisation is needed) is refused:
    <em>membership revoked</em>, or <em>roles changed; obtain a fresh token</em>. A demoted member's next heartbeat
    brings a token with the reduced roles.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cache is never consulted over the network on a request. If it has not been refreshed within its
    <code>maxStalenessMs</code> (60 s in httpeers-member), the peer refuses every token rather than guess. A stream
    that is already open is cut by <code>guardStream</code> when its caller is revoked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="unsupported">What is not implemented</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Delegation of membership to a different holder (above): a token always names, and is bound to, one subject. An application can pass its own rights on (<a href="#application-grants">above</a>).</li>
    <li>Attenuation by path or method: the verifier does not assert <code>resource</code> or <code>operation</code>.</li>
    <li>Audience by class (<code>audience_class</code>): nothing produces the facts it would check, so such a token is refused.</li>
    <li>
      Key rotation with an issuer directory and per-device binding. <code>verifyToken</code> already accepts several
      keys (<code>keys</code>), which is where rotation would plug in.
    </li>
    <li>Third-party blocks: <code>webrun-biscuit</code> implements them; httpeers-access does not use them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="examples">See it run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/examples/access-tokens">Tokens and policy</a>: roles, capabilities, a stolen token, a foreign mesh.</li>
    <li><a href="/examples/delegation">Narrowing a token</a>: attenuation, checked at another peer.</li>
    <li><a href="/examples/app-grants">Passing a right to another peer</a>: an application grant, narrowed by Alice for Carol.</li>
    <li><a href="/examples/hub-invitation">A hub in a tab, and an invitation</a>: where tokens come from.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Why the token is attached by the ServiceWorker edge, and what code may wield it, is the
    <a href="/docs/security">security model</a>. How a member gets its first token:
    <a href="/docs/membership">joining a mesh</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
